WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.370
Today, we're going to talk some more about
fluid simulations and fluid guiding.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:10.379
Hell yeah!

00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:15.950
As you know all too well, it is possible to
simulate the laws of fluid motion on a computer,

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:21.160
make a digital scene, and create absolutely
beautiful videos such as the ones you see

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.160
here.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.210
Newer and newer research papers show up to
both extend the possible scenarios that we

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.020
can simulate, and there are also other works
to speed up already existing solutions.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:37.780
This piece of work introduces a technique
that mathematicians like to call the primal-dual

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.440
optimization method.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.780
This helps us accomplish two really cool things.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.949
One is fluid guiding.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:49.940
Fluid guiding is a problem where we're looking
to exert control over the fluid while keeping

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.730
the fluid flow as natural as possible.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:57.910
I've written my Master thesis on the very
same topic and can confirm that it's one hell

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:58.930
of a problem.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:03.550
The core of the problem is that if we use
the laws of physics to create a fluid simulation,

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.759
we get what would happen in reality as a result.

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:11.540
However, if we wish to guide this piece of
fluid towards a target shape, for instance,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.259
to form an image of our choice, we have to
both retain natural fluid flows, but still,

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:22.790
create something that would be highly unlikely
to happen according to the laws of physics.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:28.570
For instance, a splash of water is unlikely
to suddenly form a human face of our choice.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.210
The proposed technique helps this ambivalent
goal of exerting a bit of control over the

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.060
fluid simulation while keeping the flows as
natural as possible.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.690
There are already many existing applications
of fluids and smoke in movies where an actor

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:48.180
fires a gun, and the fire and smoke plumes
are added to the footage in post production.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:53.719
However, with a high quality fluid guiding
technique, we could choose target shapes for

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:58.360
these smoke plumes and explosions that best
convey our artistic vision.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.250
And number two, it also accomplishes something
that we call separating boundary conditions,

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:16.750
which prevents imprecisions where small fluid
volumes are being stuck to walls.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.400
The guiding process is also followed by an
upsampling step, where we take a coarse simulation,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.020
and artificially synthesize sharp, high-frequency
details onto it.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.620
Computing the more detailed simulation would
often take days without such synthesizing

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.710
techniques.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:37.300
Kind of like with Wavelet Turbulence, which
is an absolutely incredible paper that was

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.190
showcased in none other than the very first
Two Minute Papers episode.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.310
Link is in the video description box.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.310
Don't watch it.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:46.750
It's quite embarrassing.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:50.190
And all this leads to eye-poppingly beautiful
solutions.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.190
Wow.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:53.330
I cannot get tired of this.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:59.210
In the paper, you will find much more about
breaking dams, tornado simulations, and applications

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.630
of the primal-dual optimization method.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:06.950
Normally, to remain as authentic to the source
materials as possible, I don't do any kind

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.030
of slow motion and other similar shenanigans,
but this time, I just couldn't resist it.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.560
Have a look at this, and I hope you'll like
the results.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.220
Hm hm!

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:23.540
If you feel the alluring call of fluids, I've
put some resources in the video description,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.340
including a gentle description I wrote on
the basics of fluid simulation and fluid control

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:34.540
with source code both on the CPU and GPU,
and a link to Doyub Kim's amazing book that

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.010
I am currently reading.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.010
Highly recommended.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.670
If you also have some online tutorials and
papers that helped you solidify your understanding

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:45.790
of the topic, make sure to leave a link in
